<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Elizabeth of Hungary</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1207–<a href="/years/1231">1231</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Saint Elizabeth of Hungary, Elizabeth of Thuringia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Elizabeth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> was a daughter of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrew-ii-of-hungary">Andrew II of Hungary</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gertrude-of-merania">Gertrude of Merania</a> who married Landgrave Louis IV of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a>. After his death on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> in 1227 she left the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wartburg">Wartburg</a> court, founded a hospital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marburg">Marburg</a> and devoted herself to the poor and sick under the Franciscan ideal of poverty. She died aged 24 and was canonised in 1235, becoming one of the most popular saints of medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/elizabeth-of-hungary.jpg" alt="Elizabeth of Hungary" width="320" height="391" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Simone Martini · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Simone_Martini_046.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
